Q: At WiX bundle level: How to determine Install / Uninstall action A: <p>I'd try <code>WixBundleInstalled</code>, I think it's set early enough for wixstdba. Something like:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;!-- Only continue if already installed OR productState is absent (2). --&gt;
&lt;bal:Condition Message=&quot;An existing installation was detected, setup cannot continue.&amp;#13;&amp;#10;Please uninstall existing version first!&quot;
               Condition=&quot;WixBundleInstalled OR EXISTING_FILES_STATE = 2&quot; /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>This is pretty important in all launch conditions so that the user isn't prevented from uninstalling.</p>

Q: How do I align the buttons I made using &lt;a&gt; inside of &lt;ul&gt; to be spaced evenly both vertically and horizontally? A: <p>What does it show for you?<br />
For me, it's 3 rows and 2 columns, cause it has this snippet:</p>
<pre><code>grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr 1fr;
</code></pre>

Q: How add &quot;or&quot; in switch statements? A: <p>Case-statements automatically fall through if you don't specify otherwise (by writing break). Therefore you can write</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>switch(myvar)
{
   case 2:
   case 5:
   {
      //your code
   break;
   }
// etc... 
}
</code></pre>

Q: app.config file in a C++/CLI project A: <p>In a similar vein to <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79735628/43534">Marcus' answer</a> I adopted a slightly simpler approach:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
  &lt;AppConfig&gt;app.config&lt;/AppConfig&gt;
  &lt;AutoGenerateBindingRedirects&gt;true&lt;/AutoGenerateBindingRedirects&gt;
&lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;
&lt;ItemGroup&gt;
  &lt;None Include=&quot;app.config&quot; /&gt;
  &lt;AppConfigWithTargetPath Include=&quot;$(AppConfig)&quot;&gt;
    &lt;TargetPath&gt;$(TargetFileName).config&lt;/TargetPath&gt;
  &lt;/AppConfigWithTargetPath&gt;
&lt;/ItemGroup&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>This uses your <code>app.config</code> as a template while automatically adding binding redirects and then copying the result to <code>YourApp.exe.config</code> as expected, without any additional fanfare, just like in C# projects.</p>
<p>I don't know why Microsoft don't do this by default; app.config files are still useful even for native C++ apps, not just C++/CLI or other .NET projects -- though the binding redirects are only for .NET.</p>
